1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,807
Meet RescueProof: a community food rescue platform with verifiable handoff records. It
connects businesses with surplus food to approved community organizations. This
walkthrough uses synthetic test data, not measured food rescue impact.

2
00:00:15,807 --> 00:00:29,819
Good food can go to waste when pickup coordination is fragmented. Messages alone do not
provide a consistent record of who claimed a donation or confirmed delivery. RescueProof
brings those steps into one traceable workflow.

3
00:00:29,819 --> 00:00:45,750
As a donor, we enter twelve demo bread items, an estimated weight, pickup times, storage
requirements, and allergens. The full address and contact are restricted to the donor and
successful claimant. We publish the test listing.

4
00:00:45,750 --> 00:01:00,472
Now we switch to a separate, approved recipient account and claim the available food.
After the claim succeeds, pickup information becomes visible to that organization. Regular
users do not need a crypto wallet to participate.

5
00:01:00,472 --> 00:01:16,583
The recipient generates a time limited pickup code. The donor checks that code and
confirms the handoff. The code shown here was consumed during this demonstration and
cannot authorize another pickup. This is a software test, not a physical delivery.

6
00:01:16,583 --> 00:01:32,280
The recipient checks the delivery and enters an actual received weight of two point eight
kilograms for this synthetic test. They then confirm receipt. The platform separates
reported actual weight from the donor's estimate instead of treating estimates as
completed impact.

7
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:49,346
The new batch is now completed, with separate events for listing, claim, handoff, and
receipt. Its records are still awaiting blockchain anchoring. A completed food workflow
and a confirmed blockchain transaction are distinct states, and the interface keeps that
distinction visible.

8
00:01:49,346 --> 00:02:08,072
Operational data stays in PostgreSQL, behind a Rust API and Vue interface, with Redis
supporting rate limits. A worker groups events into a Merkle root. A relayer anchors that
compact commitment on Ethereum Sepolia, reducing blockchain writes and keeping personal
details off chain.

9
00:02:08,072 --> 00:02:24,134
For on chain verification, we open a different demo batch that was anchored earlier. Its
four event records show confirmed anchors, transaction links, and a downloadable proof.
This existing proof is shown separately from the new, unanchored batch.

10
00:02:24,134 --> 00:02:39,900
Opening the linked transaction in Etherscan independently shows a successful anchor call
on Sepolia. A Merkle proof checks record integrity against the published root. It cannot
prove that food physically existed, was safe, or actually reached someone.

11
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:59,666
RescueProof aims to make food sharing easier and its records easier to check. The
prototype supports Chinese, English, French, and Japanese. Built by a solo developer, it
combines practical coordination with independently verifiable records. Explore the open
source project on GitHub.
